  "summary": "Coders, musicians and pixel artists push hardware to its bones — for glory, not money.",
  "description": "The demoscene grew from 1980s software piracy credits: cracking groups signed disk copies with animated intros, then the intros became the point. Demos are realtime, machine-limited audiovisual artworks — procedural geometry, tracker music, shader sorcery — competed at parties like Assembly (Helsinki), Revision (Saarbrücken) and The Gathering in categorized compos. Categories (64k intro, 4k intro, wild) enforce ascetic constraints; scene fame is earned by pushing a C64 or a GPU further than it should go. UNESCO-recognized in Finland, Germany and other countries as living digital culture, the scene seeded today's GPU demoscene, procedural art and much of game-engine visual culture.",
  "culturalContext": "The demoscene is Europe's most respected digital underground: Finland, Germany and other nations recognize it as living cultural heritage, demo parties (Assembly, Revision) are rite-of-passage institutions, and scene credit culture (respect earned by pushing hardware) produced generations of game-industry graphics and audio programmers.",
